declared width W = 13, column lengths [1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 1024], 64 seeds each

AXIS                        DISAGREEMENT                       VERDICT
1 headroom                      0 of    640 inputs disagree   SAME ANSWER
2 packing                       0 of    640 inputs disagree   SAME ANSWER
3 overflow policy             511 of    640 inputs disagree   DIFFERENT ANSWER
4 intermediate precision        0 of    640 inputs disagree   SAME ANSWER

FIRST DISAGREEING INPUT, PER AXIS
  1 headroom                 none
  2 packing                  none
  3 overflow policy          n=2 seed=10: 409 against 8191
  4 intermediate precision   none

WHAT THIS DECIDES

Three of `25` section 7's four axes are OBSERVABLE by `97` section 3.2's
test: moving them changes the value the program computes, so a consumer
must be told and they sit in input position. One is UNOBSERVABLE: packing
changes where the bits live and nothing else.

So `25` section 7's object is three parts policy and one part lowering,
and the cold pair's argmin-over-cost object is entirely lowering. They are
not two definitions of one thing competing on strength. They name two
different layers, with opposite polarity, and no merge was ever available.

And the unobservable one is exactly the axis the committed corpus benches:
the bitpack-* families sweep packing against dense with every arm asserted
to agree. p1 shows why that is not a coincidence.
